// examples/hydrogen/mhhc_corner_pbox.sio
//
// WHAT THE SEVEN-STAGE CASCADE PREDICTS WHEN THE THINGS WE DO NOT KNOW ARE
// CARRIED THROUGH INSTEAD OF PINNED.
//
// examples/hydrogen/mhhc_cascade.sio runs the Gkanas et al. 2020 compressor
// as a coupled cascade on the paper's measured thermodynamics and reports a
// single number: 28.1x against the paper's 18.7x. A single number from a
// model with seven unmeasured inputs is not a prediction, it is one draw from
// a set nobody has drawn the rest of.
//
// This file draws the rest of it: each unmeasured input becomes an interval,
// and the cascade runs at all 2^7 = 128 CORNERS of that box.
//
// THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FILE WAS VACUOUS, and saying how is the point of
// the paragraph. It varied six inputs -- the four kinetic constants, UA and
// V_ic -- and pinned the plateau shape at flat_plateau(), sigma = 0. With
// sigma = 0 the slope term of p_eq_pct vanishes identically, so the
// delivery pressure does not depend on loading, and therefore does not depend
// on ANY of those six. The run returned the interval [561.549, 561.549] bar
// across 64 corners: one number restated sixty-four times, under a heading
// that announced a negative result. The quantity being swept could not
// respond to the sweep. Measured, not deduced: S7's final loading moved
// across [0.000, 0.902] over those same corners while the pressure moved by
// zero digits.
//
// The fix is not cosmetic. The paper's own eq. 11 carries the slope term, and
// the data note records that the paper does not give sigma_s, sigma_0 or Y.
// Pinning sigma at zero was a choice, and it was the choice that made the
// answer independent of the question. sigma is now the seventh interval.
//
// WHY HYSTERESIS IS STILL PINNED AT ZERO, and this one is physics rather than
// oversight: the bed thermodynamics are per-direction, and the gap between
// dh_abs and dh_des already IS the hysteresis (stdlib/hydride/mhhc.sio:60).
// Adding the paper's Y term on top of measured per-direction enthalpies would
// count the same effect twice. So the box has seven inputs, not eight.
//
// WHY CORNERS AND NOT MONTE CARLO. Monte Carlo needs distributions. We do not
// have them: four of these seven are the LaNi5 kinetic constants of Jemni &
// Ben Nasrallah standing in for the paper's AB2 alloys, and there is no
// defensible density for "the value for a different alloy". An interval is
// the honest object, and for a response monotone in each input the extremes
// of a box are attained at its corners. Monotonicity is NOT assumed -- the
// corner that attained each extreme is printed, and if it is a mixed corner
// the bounds are reported as a LOWER bound on the spread rather than as the
// spread.
//
// THE SEVEN, AND WHERE EACH INTERVAL COMES FROM
//
//   c_abs, e_abs, c_des, e_des  SUBSTITUTED. LaNi5 constants used for AB2
//     stages. Prefactors get a factor of 2 either way, activation energies
//     +/-20%. These widths are JUDGEMENT, not measurement, and they are the
//     weakest declared quantity here. They are wide because the substitution
//     is the largest approximation in the model, not because anything
//     measured them.
//
//   ua    CHOSEN. Bed area 2*pi*R*L = 1.61 m2; a water-side coefficient from
//     500 to 5000 W/m2K spans UA from 806 to 8060 W/K.
//
//   v_ic  CHOSEN. The interconnector volume is not stated. 0.001 to 0.02 m3
//     spans a short large-bore manifold to a small buffer.
//
//   sigma NOT GIVEN BY THE PAPER, which states the form of eq. 11 and not
//     its constants. It is now d(ln P)/dx across the plateau, linear and
//     finite, because the tangent form of eq. 11 was replaced by an
//     explicit three-region PCT isotherm -- see the note in
//     stdlib/hydride/mhhc.sio. The interval runs from 0.0, a perfectly
//     flat plateau and still the sibling file's assumption, to 2.0, a
//     plateau whose pressure rises about fivefold from one phase boundary
//     to the other. Measured with the new isotherm: the corner that used
//     to lose 6% of its hydrogen now conserves to 0.000 ppm at every step
//     size tried, with no clamping and no adaptive control.
//
//   x_alpha, x_beta  DECLARED at 0.10 and 0.90 and held fixed rather than
//     swept, to keep this a 2^7 box. They are the loadings at which the
//     solid-solution and beta branches take over. Holding them fixed is a
//     restriction of this experiment, not a claim that they are known.
//
// WHAT IS NOT IN THE BOX. The per-stage plateau pressures come from the
// paper's measured dH and dS, and the bound "Table 3 bounds Table 4" in the
// sibling file is computed from those alone. It touches none of the seven,
// is invariant across this entire box, and nothing here can weaken it.

use hydride::mhhc::*

fn print_fix3(x: f64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    let neg = x < 0.0
    var v = if neg { 0.0 - x } else { x }
    let scaled = v * 1000.0
    var n = scaled as i64
    let frac = scaled - (n as f64)
    if frac >= 0.5 { n = n + 1 }
    let whole = n / 1000
    let milli = n - whole * 1000
    if neg { print("-") }
    print(whole) print(".")
    if milli < 100 { print("0") }
    if milli < 10 { print("0") }
    print(milli)
}

// Bit k of mask, without pow: pow with a non-literal exponent returns 0.0 on
// this toolchain (stdlib/chemistry/catalysis.sio:40 records it).
fn bit_of(mask: i64, k: i64) -> i64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var m = mask
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < k {
        m = m / 2
        i = i + 1
    }
    m - (m / 2) * 2
}

fn pick(mask: i64, k: i64, lo: f64, hi: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    if bit_of(mask, k) == 1 { hi } else { lo }
}

fn bed_for(stage: i64, ua: f64) -> HydrideBed with Mut, Div, Panic {
    gkanas_bed(stage, 419.0, 500.0, ua)
}

fn couple(stage_hot: i64, stage_cold: i64, x_hot0: f64, x_cold0: f64,
          n_ic0: f64, t_hot: f64, t_cold: f64, ua: f64, v_ic: f64,
          kin: HydrideKinetics, shape: PlateauShape,
          out: &![f64; 8], rep: &![f64; 8]) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var s0: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    s0[0] = x_hot0
    s0[1] = x_cold0
    s0[2] = n_ic0
    s0[3] = t_hot
    s0[4] = t_cold
    // STEP SIZE, established by convergence testing rather than chosen.
    // The whole cyclic steady state was run at dt = 0.02, 0.1, 0.5 and 2.0,
    // at nominal kinetics and at the slow-kinetics corner that had
    // exhausted the budget, and the delivery pressure came out 332.761 bar
    // in all eight runs -- identical to the last digit across a 100x range
    // of step, with 0.000 ppm drift and the same nine fixed-point sweeps.
    // dt = 0.02 was resolving a timescale this problem does not have.
    //
    // The cycle TIME is not equally insensitive: it moves from 9879 s to
    // 10124 s over that same 100x, which is the first-order error in the
    // time taken to REACH steady state rather than in the state itself.
    // At dt = 0.5 that error is 1.1%, against a cycle-time interval that
    // spans 2000 s to 170000 s across this box. Declared, not hidden.
    //
    // 1e6 steps at dt = 0.5 is 500000 s of simulated time per coupling; the
    // slowest corner measured needs 66000 s for the whole cascade.
    run_coupling(&s0, bed_for(stage_hot, ua), bed_for(stage_cold, ua), kin, shape,
                 v_ic, 300.0, t_hot, t_cold, 0.5, 1000000 as i64, 1.0e-10, out, rep)
}

fn abs_f(x: f64) -> f64 { if x < 0.0 { 0.0 - x } else { x } }

// One full cascade at one corner, run to CYCLIC STEADY STATE.
//
// A stage does not begin absorbing from an empty bed. It begins from whatever
// it retained when it desorbed into the stage above, so the six start-of-
// absorption loadings are a fixed point of the cascade, not constants to be
// chosen. Choosing them is what the earlier version of this file did, and it
// was wrong in a way the flat plateau hid: with a real isotherm a bed at
// exactly x = 0 has p_eq = 0, rate_abs returns zero, and NOTHING MOVES --
// measured, every stage ending at 0.000 loading and the delivery at 0 bar.
//
// Convergence measured at sigma = 2.0: monotone, geometric with ratio ~0.62,
// nine sweeps to move the largest residual under 1e-3, delivery settling from
// 311.97 bar on the first sweep to 332.76 bar. No damping needed.
//
// STAGE 7 IS NOT PART OF THE FIXED POINT, and this is a real limitation
// rather than an approximation. Stages 2 through 6 desorb into the stage
// above, so their residual is determined here. Stage 7 desorbs into the
// delivery line, and what it retains depends on that line's pressure, which
// this model does not simulate. Its start-of-absorption loading stays at the
// declared seed and is NOT self-consistent.
//
// res[0]=delivery bar, res[1]=summed time s, res[2]=S7 loading,
// res[3]=1.0 if every coupling converged, res[4]=worst inventory drift,
// res[5]=fixed-point sweeps used, res[6]=1.0 if the fixed point converged.
fn run_cascade(t_hot: f64, t_cold: f64, ua: f64, v_ic: f64,
               kin: HydrideKinetics, shape: PlateauShape, x_seed: f64,
               res: &![f64; 8]) with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var out: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var rep: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var resid: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var out0: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var q: i64 = 0
    while q < 8 { resid[q] = x_seed; q = q + 1 }

    var total_time = 0.0
    var worst_drift = 0.0
    var unconverged: i64 = 0
    var x_prev = 1.0
    var fp_converged = false
    var sweep: i64 = 0
    while sweep < 20 && !fp_converged {
        total_time = 0.0
        worst_drift = 0.0
        unconverged = 0
        x_prev = 1.0
        var k: i64 = 0
        while k < 6 {
            couple(k, k + 1, x_prev, resid[k], 0.05, t_hot, t_cold, ua, v_ic, kin, shape, &! out, &! rep)
            total_time = total_time + rep[0]
            if rep[3] > worst_drift { worst_drift = rep[3] }
            if rep[4] < 0.5 { unconverged = unconverged + 1 }
            out0[k] = out[0]
            x_prev = out[1]
            k = k + 1
        }
        var worst_change = 0.0
        var j: i64 = 0
        while j < 5 {
            let d = abs_f(out0[j + 1] - resid[j])
            if d > worst_change { worst_change = d }
            resid[j] = out0[j + 1]
            j = j + 1
        }
        sweep = sweep + 1
        if worst_change < 0.001 { fp_converged = true }
    }

    let p_deliver = p_eq_pct(gkanas_dh_des(6), gkanas_ds_des(6), t_hot, x_prev, shape, false)
    res[0] = p_deliver
    res[1] = total_time
    res[2] = x_prev
    res[3] = if unconverged == 0 { 1.0 } else { 0.0 }
    res[4] = worst_drift
    res[5] = sweep as f64
    res[6] = if fp_converged { 1.0 } else { 0.0 }
}

fn main() -> i32 with Mut, Div, Panic, IO {
    println("SEVEN-STAGE MH COMPRESSOR: CORNER P-BOX OVER SEVEN UNMEASURED INPUTS")
    println("Gkanas et al. 2020, Case 1, 10 -> 80 C. 2^7 = 128 corners.")
    println("")
    println("the paper measures 374 bar delivery, ratio 18.7, cycle 6625 s")
    println("the sibling file's flat-plateau run gives 561.5 bar, ratio 28.1,")
    println("and that run is corner 0 of this box (sigma = 0 is its low end)")
    println("")

    let t_cold = 283.15
    let t_hot = 353.15

    // interval endpoints, in the order the mask bits address them
    let c_abs_lo = 29.594     let c_abs_hi = 118.374
    let e_abs_lo = 16943.68   let e_abs_hi = 25415.52
    let c_des_lo = 4.785      let c_des_hi = 19.140
    let e_des_lo = 13136.0    let e_des_hi = 19704.0
    let ua_lo = 806.0         let ua_hi = 8060.0
    let v_ic_lo = 0.001       let v_ic_hi = 0.020
    // sigma is now d(ln P)/dx, LINEAR across the plateau -- not the
    // coefficient of a tangent. The interval had to be rewidened when the
    // isotherm changed: 0.25 in the old tangent form was a strong slope,
    // but as a linear coefficient it moves the plateau pressure by only
    // exp(0.25 * 0.8) = 1.22x from one phase boundary to the other, which
    // is very nearly flat. Sweeping that would have been the same vacuity
    // as before wearing different clothes. A plateau that rises by a
    // factor 2 to 5 across its width -- the range real AB2 and AB5 alloys
    // show -- is d(ln P)/dx between 0.87 and 2.0 over x_beta - x_alpha =
    // 0.8, so the interval runs from 0 (perfectly flat, still the sibling
    // file's assumption and still corner 0) to 2.0.
    let sigma_lo = 0.0        let sigma_hi = 2.0

    var res: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]

    var p_min = 1.0e30
    var p_max = 0.0 - 1.0e30
    var t_min = 1.0e30
    var t_max = 0.0 - 1.0e30
    var x_min = 1.0e30
    var x_max = 0.0 - 1.0e30
    var p_min_mask: i64 = 0 - 1
    var p_max_mask: i64 = 0 - 1
    var converged_corners: i64 = 0
    var dropped_corners: i64 = 0
    var drift_rejected: i64 = 0
    var worst_drift = 0.0
    var worst_drift_mask: i64 = 0 - 1
    var worst_kept_drift = 0.0
    var brackets: i64 = 0
    var fp_min: i64 = 999
    var fp_max: i64 = 0
    var fp_failed: i64 = 0
    // where the conservation failures sit, by the sigma bit, because a
    // rejection that correlates with one input is a lead and an uncorrelated
    // one is noise
    var rej_sigma_hi: i64 = 0
    var rej_sigma_lo: i64 = 0

    var mask: i64 = 0
    while mask < 128 {
        let kin = HydrideKinetics {
            c_abs: pick(mask, 0, c_abs_lo, c_abs_hi),
            e_abs: pick(mask, 1, e_abs_lo, e_abs_hi),
            c_des: pick(mask, 2, c_des_lo, c_des_hi),
            e_des: pick(mask, 3, e_des_lo, e_des_hi),
        }
        let ua = pick(mask, 4, ua_lo, ua_hi)
        let v_ic = pick(mask, 5, v_ic_lo, v_ic_hi)
        let shape = pct_plateau(pick(mask, 6, sigma_lo, sigma_hi), 0.0, 0.10, 0.90)

        run_cascade(t_hot, t_cold, ua, v_ic, kin, shape, 0.10, &! res)

        if res[4] > worst_drift { worst_drift = res[4]  worst_drift_mask = mask }

        // THREE conditions, not two. The first version of this file floored on
        // convergence and on interval width, PRINTED the inventory drift, and
        // used it for nothing -- and the run came back with a worst drift of
        // 720983 ppm, 72% of the hydrogen unaccounted for, inside an interval
        // reported as a result. A corner that loses mass is not a value of the
        // model regardless of whether its residual stopped changing. The
        // nominal case conserves to 0.000 ppm, so 1 ppm is a generous floor.
        let conserves = res[4] <= 1.0e-6
        if res[3] < 0.5 {
            dropped_corners = dropped_corners + 1
        } else if !conserves {
            drift_rejected = drift_rejected + 1
            if bit_of(mask, 6) == 1 { rej_sigma_hi = rej_sigma_hi + 1 } else { rej_sigma_lo = rej_sigma_lo + 1 }
        } else {
            converged_corners = converged_corners + 1
            if res[4] > worst_kept_drift { worst_kept_drift = res[4] }
            if res[0] < p_min { p_min = res[0]  p_min_mask = mask }
            if res[0] > p_max { p_max = res[0]  p_max_mask = mask }
            if res[1] < t_min { t_min = res[1] }
            if res[1] > t_max { t_max = res[1] }
            if res[2] < x_min { x_min = res[2] }
            if res[2] > x_max { x_max = res[2] }
            if res[0] <= 374.0 { brackets = brackets + 1 }
            let sw = res[5] as i64
            if sw < fp_min { fp_min = sw }
            if sw > fp_max { fp_max = sw }
            if res[6] < 0.5 { fp_failed = fp_failed + 1 }
        }
        mask = mask + 1
    }

    println("-- corner accounting --")
    print("  usable (converged AND conserving): ") print(converged_corners) println(" of 128")
    print("  dropped, never reached steady state: ") print(dropped_corners) println("")
    print("  dropped, inventory drift above 1 ppm: ") print(drift_rejected) println("")
    if drift_rejected > 0 {
        print("    of those, ") print(rej_sigma_hi) print(" at sigma=hi and ")
        print(rej_sigma_lo) println(" at sigma=lo")
        print("  worst drift anywhere in the box: ") print_fix3(worst_drift * 1000000.0)
        print(" ppm, at corner ") print(worst_drift_mask) println("")
    }
    print("  worst drift among the corners KEPT: ") print_fix3(worst_kept_drift * 1000000.0) println(" ppm")
    println("")

    println("-- the box, over the USABLE corners only --")
    print("  delivery pressure  [") print_fix3(p_min) print(", ") print_fix3(p_max) println("] bar")
    print("  compression ratio  [") print_fix3(p_min / 20.0) print(", ") print_fix3(p_max / 20.0) println("]")
    print("  summed coupling t  [") print_fix3(t_min) print(", ") print_fix3(t_max) println("] s")
    print("  S7 final loading   [") print_fix3(x_min) print(", ") print_fix3(x_max) println("]")
    print("  worst drift (kept) ") print_fix3(worst_kept_drift * 1000000.0) println(" ppm")
    print("  fixed-point sweeps [") print(fp_min) print(", ") print(fp_max) println("]")
    print("  corners whose fixed point did NOT converge: ") print(fp_failed) println("")
    println("")

    // FLOORS COME AFTER THE REPORT, not before. The previous version
    // returned on a refusal without printing the box, so the refusal hid
    // the very numbers that explained it -- a separate probe had to be
    // written to recover what the program already knew. A floor should
    // refuse to CONCLUDE, never refuse to REPORT.
    // ANTI-VACUITY FLOORS. Three ways this can look like an answer
    // without being one. The first version of this file hit two of them and
    // the second hit the third.
    if converged_corners < 96 {
        println("REFUSED: fewer than three quarters of the corners are usable.")
        println("An interval over the rest is an interval over whichever corners")
        println("happened to integrate cleanly, which is not the box. Either the")
        println("integrator needs work at those corners or the intervals are")
        println("wider than the model can be run over -- both are findings, and")
        println("neither is a compression ratio.")
        return 1
    }
    if p_max - p_min < 1.0e-6 {
        println("REFUSED: the delivery pressure did not move across the box. The")
        println("swept inputs cannot reach the reported quantity -- this is the")
        println("failure the header describes, and it is an error, not a result.")
        return 1
    }

    println("-- against the paper's measured 374 bar --")
    if p_min <= 374.0 {
        if p_max >= 374.0 {
            println("  374 bar lies INSIDE the box. The declared ignorance spans the")
            println("  measurement, so the sibling file's 1.5x gap is one corner of a")
            println("  box that also contains the paper's value -- not a refutation of")
            println("  the model and not a confirmation of it either. What it says is")
            println("  that these seven inputs must be MEASURED before this cascade")
            println("  predicts anything at this precision.")
            print("  corners at or below the measurement: ") print(brackets)
            print(" of ") print(converged_corners) println("")
        } else {
            println("  the box lies ENTIRELY BELOW 374 bar: the model underpredicts")
            println("  everywhere, which the flat-plateau run did not show.")
        }
    } else {
        println("  the box lies ENTIRELY ABOVE 374 bar. The overprediction survives")
        println("  every combination of the seven, so it is not parameter ignorance:")
        println("  it is the model form or a mechanism the model omits.")
        print("  closest approach: ") print_fix3(p_min) print(" bar, ")
        print_fix3(p_min / 374.0) println("x the measurement")
    }
    println("")

    println("-- is the corner set sufficient? --")
    print("  minimum attained at corner ") print(p_min_mask)
    if p_min_mask == 0 { println("  (all-low, consistent with monotone)") } else { println("  (mixed: NOT monotone in all seven)") }
    print("  maximum attained at corner ") print(p_max_mask)
    if p_max_mask == 127 { println("  (all-high, consistent with monotone)") } else { println("  (mixed: NOT monotone in all seven)") }
    println("  Extremes at mixed corners mean monotonicity fails and the true")
    println("  range can exceed this box: these are then a LOWER bound on the")
    println("  spread. Reported, not hidden.")
    println("")

    println("MHHC_CORNER_PBOX_OK")
    0
}
